course: dictionary
lesson: 16151
title: Many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word wings can mean different things. A bird, a bat, and an aeroplane all have wings, but the wings are not the same. Look at the words around wings to find out which meaning the writer means.
explain: «The eagle spread its wings and soared over the valley.» Here the eagle is alive, so wings are the body parts it flies with. «The plane lifted off, its wings glinting in the sun.» Here there is no animal, so wings must be the long flat parts on the sides of a plane.
explain: Sometimes wings is used in a saying. «Mum took the new girl under her wing» does not mean anyone grew feathers. It means Mum looked after her. Ask yourself: does this sentence make sense with the real body part, or is it a picture or an idea?
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Wings are the body parts a bird, bat or insect moves to fly.
fact: Wings are the long flat parts sticking out from the sides of an aeroplane.
fact: A wing can also be a part of a building that sticks out from the main part, and the wings of a stage are the hidden sides where actors wait.
fact: In sports, the wings are the sides of the pitch, and a winger plays there.
exercise: «The eagle spread its wings and soared.» What are the wings in this sentence? | Long flat parts of an aeroplane | The hidden sides of a stage | *Body parts a bird uses to fly | The sides of a football pitch
exercise: «The actors waited in the wings until their cue.» Where were the actors waiting? | Under the seats in the front row | *In the hidden sides of the stage | On the roof of the theatre | Out in the car park
exercise: Mum said, «Take the new girl under your wing.» What does this saying mean? | *Look after her | Fly her to school | Hide her from view | Tell her to go away
test: «The bat's wings are thin skin stretched over long fingers.» What are the bat's wings made of? | Wood and cloth | *Skin stretched over long fingers | Feathers | Thin metal
test: «The winger raced down the wing and crossed the ball.» Where did the winger run? | *Along the side of the pitch | Under the bench | Along the top row of the seats | Through the middle of the goal
exam: «The new library is in the west wing of the school.» What does wing mean here? | The long flat part of a plane | A body part a bird uses to fly | The sides of a stage where actors wait | *A part of a building that sticks out from the main part
